Message-ID: <DqFK28.LJ9@root.co.uk>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 151506867
references: <h4lojowbdg.fsf@dbsrv.gsfc.nasa.gov> <4lid0q$itq@jik.israel.net>
organization: UniSoft Ltd., London, England
newsgroups: comp.unix.programmer,comp.unix.osf.misc,comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.lang.c


I'm surprised nobody has mentioned the following important point yet:

The POSIX no-leap-second restriction only applies to gmtime(), and to
localtime() and mktime() when used with TZ=UCT0.  It allows time-related
functions other than gmtime() to account for leap seconds when TZ has
other values (such as TZ=GMT0BST, or I think even with TZ=GMT0).

-- 
Geoff Clare <gwc@root.co.uk>
UniSoft Limited, London, England.



