In article <317FA91F.1FEA@fwg.ki.eunet.de>, "H. S. R�pcke" <fwg@fwg.ki.eunet.de> writes:
|>
	<stuff deleted>

|>in /sbin/it.d/data/objects some scripts are startet
|>and for example a licence (twouser) is integrated in
|>the system by using lmf. But LMF is startet in the
|>following rc3-section! So at this point LMF isnt
|>running to take inputs from it.
|>
|>How is this riddle solved?
|>
|>Roepcke
|>

LMF doesn't need to be "running" until somebody is ready to check licenses.
The "it" script is simply registering license information into the LMF license
database.  Subsequently, the init script that starts lmf issues a "reset" which
reads the license database into the kernel's LMF cache.  From this point on,
lmf calls can be made by applications as the licenses are known to the system.

No riddle at all!

Dave

