V3.0 Tech Overview (Aug 94) says:
The default virtual memory per process is 2GB.....

....This value can be increased to a maximum of 8 TB by setting the MAXVAS 
variable.....

Actually if you are running V3.2 or higher, you can edit the /etc/sysconfigtab 
file to include someting like: [for example]

vm:
[TAB]   vm-maxvas = 1099511627776

Then reboot the system. This would up your system's max virtual adress-space 
to 1 TB [note this is per process, welcome to 64-bits]

Also don't forget that depending on what you do, that you might need to fully 
back this with SWAP space !

We definitely need to be able to adress more than 1 or 2 GB with a single 
process, since we are running for example Oracle7 with SGA's (system global 
areas) in the 6-8GB range. Those SGA's are shared memory regions mapped my 
multiple Oracle processes.


