From mcintosh@zk3.dec.com Tue Sep 27 04:23:24 1994
Date: Mon, 26 Sep 94 16:14:31 -0400
From: "Steve McIntosh, DEC OSF/1 Product Marketing" <mcintosh@zk3.dec.com>
To: cgi@crl.com
Cc: mcintosh@zk3.dec.com
Subject: spin locks from Paula Long

Hi,

  There are multiple ways to construct a spin lock using the existing 
  primitives in DEC OSF/1.  One can use the BSB V4.4 semaphores or the
  Realtime (POSIX) semaphores.  One could also use the msem_tas()
  function (test and set, and shared memory to construct your own).

  Remember that spin locks are only really "goodness" on an MP configuration.
  You probably want to use blocking locks on uni processor configuration.

  I personally would develop a generic set of locks which would work in
  either environment.  It's probably easier to do this with either the
  BSD V4.4 semaphores, msem_*() or the Realtime Semaphores, sem_*().
  Note, the code fragments below are just that frags, you probably want
  to customize things for your environment. I also believe spin_locks
  with no exit hatch are probably dangerous, because you could wedge
  the system.

Paula

==========================================================================

Options:
  The _msem_tas() function is a simple assembler routine which performs
  an atomic test-and-set.  It expects as input a pointer to an int.
  
   extern int _msem_tas(int *lock);

  It returns a 0 if update is successful , 1 if it's not. 

  A simple spin lock using this technique might look as follows (assumes no
  simple lock for none MP case):


Init Semaphores: 

  < create a shared memory region, using either mmap() or system V
  < shared memory. and make sure your semaphores lives here
     
  processors_in_box = getsysinfo((GSI_CPUS_IN_BOX,maxcpu,sizeof(maxcpu),0,0);
  spin_count = (processors_in_box - 1) * spins; /*unclear what this should be */


Lock Routine:
/*
 * attempt to get the lock, spin_count times.   If you don't win the lock, 
 * yield to another process  and try again later.  In a uni-processor case 
 * you might always yield in the lock lost case.
 */

void
spin_lock(int *lock) {
     int spins;
 
     spins = spin_count; 
     while (_msem_tas(lock) !=0 ) {   
	if (!(spins--)) {
	   <block/yield> 
	   spins = spin_count;
        }
     }
}

/* 
 * release the lock.
 */

void
spin_unlock(int *lock) {
      mb();		/* order exit from critical section */
      *lock = 0;	/* release lock	                    */
      mb();		/* order release of lock            */
}

BSD V4.4 Semaphore:

This case is similar to the msem_tas() case except, that you actually
have a semaphore you can block on and be woken-up on.  The Realtime
Semaphore model would look about the same except that you'd use the
sem_trywait function to spin with.

Init Semaphores: 

  Create Semaphores (see msem_init man page)
 
  processors_in_box = getsysinfo((GSI_CPUS_IN_BOX,maxcpu,sizeof(maxcpu),0,0);
  spin_count = (processors_in_box - 1) * spins; /*unclear what this should be */


/* 
 * lock (doesn't handel errors correctly (you could -1 for more then
 * just you didn't win the lock).
 */

void
spin_lock(msemaphore *sem) {

	int spins=0;
        spins = spin_count;
	while (msem_lock(sem,MSEM_IF_NOWAIT) == -1) {
	   if (!(spins--))
		msem_lock(sem,0);
        }
}

For the unlock case, simply call the msem_unlock() function directly.

