In article <317E36D5.3C8E41C3@mit.edu>, David Soleau Lum <davknav@mit.edu> writes:
|>Hi.
|>
|>I just upgraded my system to Digital Unix 3.2 (uname -a ---> OSF1
|>mae.mit.edu V3.2 148 alpha), and I can no longer mount CDs as "cdfs"
|>type file systems!
|>
|>Mounting installation CDs from DEC still works, as they have UFS
|>filesystems on them.  However, trying to mount such disks as "DEC
|>Freeware" (which worked prior to the OS update) no fails.  Here's an
|>example of what happens when I successfully mount a UFS disk and fail to
|>mount a CDFS disk:
|>
|>WORKS:
|>#/sbin/mount -r -v -t ufs /dev/rz4c /mnt
|>-o ro
|>/dev/rz4c on /mnt type ufs (ro)
|>
|>FAILS:
|># /sbin/mount -r -v -t cdfs /dev/rz4c /mnt
|>-o ro
|>/dev/rz4c on /mnt: No such device
|>
|>
|>I conclude from this that my kernel no longer comprehends the CDFS file
|>system, but this is just a guess.  Any suggestions on what's wrong?  If
|>my diagnosis is correct, how can I easily add CDFS support to the
|>kernel?
|>-- 

Correct.  As kernel configuration files cannot be guaranteed to work from one
release to the next (options change, config entries may change, etc) a new
configuration file is created.  Unfortunately, it doesn't include any of the
additional kernel options you may have included previously.  

Simply rebuild your kernel using "doconfig" and be sure to select the kernel
option for CDFS.  Then boot off the new kernel.

For version V4.0, the update supports an option which will let you select your
kernel options during the update.

Dave
