In article <Dq2DDn.MoD@plato.ds.boeing.com>, John Haro <haro@fclzoo.itdl.ds.boeing.com> writes:

>I need some kind of way to do a listing on my DEC TZ87 tape drive to find out
>how many and number of dumpfiles so I know how much tape I have left.

You could start with:

% dd if=/dev/nrmt#h of=/dev/null bs=block-size-of-dump

Repeat until end of tape.  Each dd(1) command will print the
number of tape records read.  Multiply by the block size and
you know how much uncompressed tape capacity has been used.
But, there's the problem; UNCOMPRESSED tape capacity.  It may
be hard to get the running compression ratio (the drive may
keep it in a mode page).  Without knowing how well the data
compresses (since the TZ87 does compression), you won't be
able to tell accurately how much more data a tape can hold.

Dump also writes this information to /dev/tty.  Depending on how
your script is written, it may be possible to capture that
information.

