David Yuan (dyuan@win20.rch.mci.com) wrote:
: hi,
: 
: I am using an Alphaserver 2100 4/275 which has 1 gb memory,
: 1 21064 CPU. OS is Digital UNIX v3.2c.  I found that P1003.1b
: named semaphore operations (sem_create, sem_wait, sem_post) are 
: faster than System V implementation (semget,semop). The following
: is the result of pure semaphore acquire and release operations 
: (at test time, the system load is very low, actually, only myself 
: was using the machine):
: 
: iterations   Posix      Sys V
: 1000000      3.544193   18.788549  # normal process
: 1000000      3.867264   18.703296  # change priority to max priority

If your multi-task application will have few lock collisions or you 
don't need the counting part of semaphores, then why not use 
spin locks on shared mem between all tasks?

DEC Has a nice wrapper for test and set: _msem_tas(), and when
combined with, memory barrier, mb(), makes for very fast SMP, multi-task
binary lock.

