In article <4lgcvc$r89@rockall.cc.strath.ac.uk>,
    Alan Stewart <A.Stewart@bell.ac.uk> writes:
|>How do you get the machine to run a command every time it boots up,
|>I am familiar with Ultrix and it's rc.local file is there an equivilant in 
|>OSF.

On Digital UNIX the most straightforward thing to do would be to
create a script in directory /sbin/rc3.d with a filename of the
form S<script_name>.  In the course of booting to full multiuser
mode the script will be called with the single argument "START".
You can determine its order in the startup procedure by adding
digits after the initial S in the filename.

This is just the most crude method of achieving your goal...for the
full description of customizing the system startup, check out the
System Administration guide (Chapter 4 in the V3.0 doc set).  Good luck!

Dan Harrington



