Richard A. Surwilo wrote:
> Our application is a very high volume message switching system and each
> process currently utilizes thousands of locks. UNIX semaphores would appear
> to be too inefficient. Using UNIX file/record locking has been suggested
> but I think it too would exact too much of a performance penalty.
> 
> Is there a library of low-level, granular, atomic locking routines
> available on an Alpha running UNIX? SVR4 compliance is immaterial. It just
> needs to be fast.

From the TruCluster Software for Digital UNIX SPD:
{
 Distributed Lock Manager

 The Distributed Lock Manager (DLM) synchronizes access to the resources
 that are shared among cooperating processes throughout the cluster.
 DLM provides a software library that applications use to implement a
 resource sharing policy. DLM provides services to notify a process 
 owning a resource that it is blocking another process requesting the
 resource. An application can also use DLM routines to coordinate the
 application's activities efficiently with the state of the cluster.
}

I imagine that the DLM wouldn't be terribly slow, plus you get the other 
advantages of a cluster (memory channel, distributed raw disk, etc).

See http://www.unix.digital.com/trucluster.html for more.

If you want something more basic, the ARM contains examples of how to use Load 
Locked/Store Conditional (LDx_L/STx_C) instructions and the Memory Barrier (MB) 
instruction to create your own locking mechanisms.
-- 
