In article <4ledut$nql@news1.h1.usa.pipeline.com> rsurwilo@usa.pipeline.com(Richard A. Surwilo) writes:

  Richard> Our application is a very high volume message switching
  Richard> system and each process currently utilizes thousands of
  Richard> locks. UNIX semaphores would appear to be too
  Richard> inefficient. Using UNIX file/record locking has been
  Richard> suggested but I think it too would exact too much of a
  Richard> performance penalty.
 
  Richard> Is there a library of low-level, granular, atomic locking
  Richard> routines available on an Alpha running UNIX? SVR4
  Richard> compliance is immaterial. It just needs to be fast.
 
Wouldn't msem_init()/msem_lock()/msem_unlock() together with a shared
memory segment do what you want?  I haven't looked at exactly how
they're implemented, but msem_lock() does call a library internal
function called _msem_tas() which uses the load linked/store
conditional instructions of the Alpha to do an atomic test-and-set.
Of course, if you don't mind Alpha assembly, you could just use loaded
linked/store conditional directly.

