Richard Westerik wrote:
> 
> We have to set the font size to an extremely small value to fit any
> window on the screen and lots of X applications expect a much larger
> screen so are either (a) partly off-screen or (b) with difficult-to-read
> fonts.
> 

I'll leave the hardware issue to the hw folks, but you can get the
xserver to draw a given font differently by specifying the -dpi argument
on the command line that starts the xserver. On a system using the x
daemon (xdm... most Decwindows systems do this), edit the last line in
the /usr/lib/X11/xdm/Xservers file to change the dpi, which by default
is 100, to 75.

I do this under CDE on 21'' monitors on an Alphastation just to get
decent lucida fonts. The CDE Xservers file is under /usr/dt/config.

The net result is that, for instance, an 18 point font which shows up
rather large on a 100dpi display shows smaller and more compact on a
75dpi display. I haven't figured out why going to LESS dots per inch
makes this occur... if anyone can explain that, I'd like to learn.



