Hi.

I've installed ssh on our AlphaStation/AlphaServer cluster.  We're running
OSF/1 v3.2a.  I installed ssh because I needed a secure way to get
passwordless root rlogin, for doing rdist and such securely.  Another big
reason I wanted it installed was to do rdumps from the 20 workstations
onto the server.  Unfortunately, OSF/1's rdump uses the rexec() system call
to run rmt on the server, rather than using rsh to make the connection.  As
a result, I still need insecure root .rhosts on the server in order to do
rdumps.  This leaves me with the problem of either poor security or no
backups.

Does anyone have any suggestions to get around this?  I'm fairly sure
dump/restore are always vendor-specific, or at least filesystem-type specific,
which is the same this in this case.  I think GNU tar comes with its own rmt,
but OSF's rdump wiull still call it with rexec().

Thanks,
Jim


