to: Richard Westerik <richardw%tiger.xs4all.nl@us3rmc.pa.dec.com>
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: Digital Equipment (Australia)
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1
x-mailer: Mozilla 2.01 (X11; I; OSF1 V3.2 alpha)

Richard Westerik wrote:
> 
> We have been installing several AlphaServer 400, 1000, 2000 and 2100
> systems. The screen resolution of the default configuration of the
> AlphaServer 1000 (Cirrus VGA 640*480 on a 14" monitor) has bad resolution
> for X. We have to set the font size to an extremely small value to fit any
> window on the screen and lots of X applications expect a much larger
> screen so are either (a) partly off-screen or (b) with difficult-to-read
> fonts.
> 
> Is there any way to increase the resolution of the default graphics card
> in the AlphaServer 1000? If so, is it possible to do it by software or do
> you need to configure/strap the card?
> 
> Thanks in advance
> Richard
> --
> .------------------------- Richard M.J. Westerik 

Richard,

Yes, there is a simple way to increase the resolution on an AlphaServer
1000. The default graphics controller i.e. the motherboard installed
Cirrus chip that I assume you are using from your message, supports
800x600 resolution as well as the default 640x480. To enable the higher
resolution just change the parameters
 used to start the X server
I.e. specifically edit the file '/usr/lib/X11/xdm/Xservers'. Add the
parameter "-screen 800" to the end of the last line wehich starts 'X'.
The line should now look something like

":0 local /usr/bin/X11/X -nice -2 -screen 800"

Hope this helps

Dave B.



