> DECunix does not support remote print filters, so you have a problem
> with your HP Laserjet, because all HP printers doesn't convert
> (unix) <CR> to <CR><LF>.

This is almost correct. The one missing bit is that you can specify 
an "rp" value of "text" while installing a non-postscript queue. The
"rp" should be "raw" for the postscript queue.

> 
> Solutions:
> 
> 1. You write a printfilter for your own, printing to a second
>    queue (1. Queue for filtering, second for printer)

An alias for 'lpr' that sends text to text and postcript to the 
raw queue is another possibility.

> 
> 2. Connect the printer via LAT (Terminalserver) or direct to the
>    machine.
> 
> If you have a jetdirect network card, you'll have another problem:
> The card doesn't work properly with any other hosts, except HP UX.
> If you send big files to the printer, the card will truncate all your
> jobs!

This is not true , although I can understand why a person might think
that way.  The HP/jet direct does a form of flow control that the lpr
in DUX3.2 did not understand. Even one page of image was enough to send 
the printer into an orgy of paper spitting as it coughed and choked.
On examining the lpr log one would find that the printer was complaining
but that DUX did not understand the code. However the lpr in 3.2D *does* 
work as does Ultrix 4.4 , SCO ODT 5.0 (and 3.0) as well as Win95.

Charlie

