[cc'd to poster]


With <4kn6b1$m38@atlas.tncnet.com>,
it seems Jay Olson (jjo@triton.com) sez:

+ I have a (Tk/Tcl) application which needs to find the times
+ when 'at' jobs of other users are to be run. I can find the
+ jobs by looking in /var/spool/cron/atjobs, but I don't know
+ when they are going to run.

The filenames themselves define the job characteristics.  Check out
the man page for at(1):

#  
#    Job numbers are specified as follows:
#  
#    user.xxxxxxxxx.y
#  
#    The user argument identifies the user who scheduled the job.  xxxxxxxxx is
#    a 9-digit number (encoded time for the job).  y indicates the job type or
#    queue name as follows:
#  
#    Argument  Job Type
#  
#    a         at job
#  
#    b         batch job
#  
#    e         ksh job
#  
#    f         csh job
#  

If you look inside the directory, you'll find that the job files
are of that type.  Personally, I wouldn't be surprised if the
"encoded time" wasn't simply the result of a call to time(2), or
maybe some ctime conversion with the seconds field stripped.


Luck++;
Phil

