In article <4kngdp$bcm@sue.cc.uregina.ca>, MAHENDRA@Meena.CC.URegina.CA (A. Mahendra Rajah) writes:
|>
|>Hello:
|>
|>   We have an Alpha 2000 running DEC Unix v3.2C. All the disks are on
|>   a RAID and are configured JBODS.
|>
|>   We have installed ADVFSBIN350 and ADVFS350 components onto the system.
|>   We also have the license ADVFS-UTILITIES installed.
|>
|>   Currently all the file systems are ufs. We wanted to convert them to
|>   AdvFS. So, we dismounted one disk and issued the command:
|>
|>   mkfdmn /dev/re7c bdomain07
|>
|>   This aborts with the message:
|>
|>   mkfdmn: domain init error E_ADVFS_NOT_INSTALLED (-1108)
|>   mkfdmn: can't create new domain 'bdomain07'
|>    
|>   What does this error mean?
|>
|>   We were able to do this on a disk that was mounted inside a
|>   DEC3000/400 without RAID.
|>
|>   Could we assume then that RAID and AdvFS do not mix?
|>
|>   Any suggestions, please?
|>
-- 
It's likely that you've failed to rebuild the kernel after loading the AdvFS
subsets.  The error message refers the kernel component of AdvFS not being
"installed" - i.e. built into the kernel.

Use doconfig to rebuild your kernel.  When presented with the list of kernel
options, be sure to select the option for AdvFS.  After you build the kernel
and reboot from it, you should have better luck.

Dave

