Ryan Thomas McBride (rtmcbrid@sfu.ca) wrote:
: I just got a DE 2000-300 and (my firstunix system) and I neet to set up 
: the C2 security. I install Digital Unix (v3.2D) and the first thibng I do 
: is run secsetup or whatever and set up enhanced security. I do shutdown 
: -r and when the login screen pops up, it won't accept my root password. 
: What am I doing wrong??

Is your root password longer than 8 characters?  If so, try logging in with
just the first 8.  Under base security, only the first 8 characters of the
password are used (the rest are ignored) to generate the encrypted password.
This encrypted password gets moved to the protected passowrd database when
you go to Enhanced Security.  However, Enhanced uses the entire password
string to generate the encrypted password.  If this is longer than 8
characters, it naturally generates a different encrypted string than just
the first 8 characters do.

From the man page for passwd, part of the Security Note:

       If a password longer than 8 character was entered under base security
       and then enhanced security is installed, you must use only the first 8
       characters of the original password.  This is because base security
       only used the first 8 characters of the password and the
       enhanced/extended password is created from the base password.

Martin
