In article <4jouon$k4@jungle.toppoint.de>, panther@jungle.toppoint.de (Hendrik S. Roepcke) writes:
|>Hello,
|>
|>very often during compling a software package, I get
|>problems with missing definitions or incompatible
|>calls.
|>
|>What is about this /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/cfe ???

It's the resident C compiler on your system.
If you compile -v, you should notice some sequence such as the
following (reformatted):

   > cc -v calc.c -o calc
  /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/cfe -DLANGUAGE_C -Dunix -D__LANGUAGE_C__ 
    -D__unix__ -D__osf__ -D__alpha -D_LONGLONG -D_CFE -DSYSTYPE_BSD
   -D_SYSTYPE_BSD -I/usr/include3.11 -Xv -Xg0 -Xdollar -O1 -std0 
   -XS/tmp/ctmstaayDta calc.c > /tmp/ctmfaayDta
 
  /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/ugen -v -G 8 -g0 -O1 /tmp/ctmfaayDta
    -o /tmp/ctmcaayDta -t /tmp/ctmstaayDta -temp /tmp/ctmgtaayDta

  /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/as1 -v -G 8 -p0 -g0 -O1 /tmp/ctmcaayDta 
    -o calc.o -t /tmp/ctmstaayDta

  /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/ld -o calc -g0 -O1 -call_shared
    /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/crt0.o calc.o -lc

First, the "cc" driver invokes /usr/lib/cmplrs/cc/cfe on calc.c,
(the acc C front-end), and tells it to generate a temp file
consisting of internal compiler format into /tmp .

Then "cc" tells the ugen phase to process that temp file into
assembler format.  Then "cc" tells the assembler (as1) to finally
generate a calc.o .  Finally "cc" instructs the linker (ld) to
wrap everything up into executable image calc.

Exercise to the reader: check out the difference in behavior if
you also specify -migrate on your original "cc" commandline.  This
has the effect of telling the driver to use DEC C, instead of acc.
DEC C is in the process of supplanting acc as the default compiler
on Digital Unix.

Tom
