organization: Memorial University of Newfoundland
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Earlier I wrote:
> It's difficult to fault DEC because someone else's code is difficult
> to support.

stannenb@emerald.tufts.edu (Saul Tannenbaum) writes:
>If Digital could not get this working, that's fine. But then, they
>should not have said it worked, or should have listed the constraints
>somewhere. They did not. It would appear that they shipped this software
>without testing in a certain environments. Again, I can't expect them
>to test everything everywhere, but we did have specific discussions
>about NIS/C2 in the "university" environment, without a single hint
>that they were going to withdraw support.

>_That_ I blame Digital for.

Agreed.  But that's a different story.  The original post showed an
apparently official disclaimer about the software.  What _does_ the
original documentation say about support of large user bases?
Does anyone know what large installations are beta-testing Digital
UNIX?

For our part, we did try to use NIS to distribute the password file.
Initially, our mail server exported the /usr/spool/mail and didn't need
authentication on the server itself.  But the performance was intolerable
and we've switched to regular transfers of a master password file since
the password file is relative static anyway.  We're now running a POP
server on the mail server, so we also need authentication.

I also deal with the problems of a large user base and the DEC's C2 GUI
interfaces were a particular problem for us.  Our first experiments
with XIsso (under V2.X) were with a thousand users.  It took over
thirty minutes to get the GUI to pull up the record for one user--this
made the GUI effectively worthless.  (Apparently the first load of the
entire database took a half hour, but after that, response improved.)
After OSF/1 V3.0, the initial load was faster--about two minutes for
1500 users.  We're now running with 10,000 users, so interactive
interfaces are just not useful to us, even if the software didn't have
its own problems.

I wrote my own software to manipulate the TCB database, some written in
C, but mostly written in perl.  We've pushed for documentation and and
enhancements and it appears that V4.0 will give a command line
interface, so we can write "compatible" shell scripts to manipulate the
database.

Creating a large user base, for test purposes, is quite simple and
should have been part of the test suite before the software ever left
engineering.

The same goes for range testing on uses of uid_t and gid_t.  In some
cases, software places arbitrary limits on the ranges in user
interfaces.  Perhaps, the programmers were unsure of the limits and
placed safety checks in the code without doing the research to see what
the real limits are.  POSIX doesn't define the limits, anyway.  Does
anyone know what the NFS limitations are?

Paul Fardy
