Curtis Williams (cwilliam@teir.com) wrote:

: Not sure if this will work for you, but if you have an extra hard disk
: or if your os original media is a cdrom, you can usually interrupt the
: normal boot sequence and boot from an alternate device. You can then
: mount the old root device arbitrarily in the file system and go into
: the old /etc/passwd file and delete root's password. Then reboot from
: the original boot device and log in as root (no password required).

: Hope this helps

This seems like a lot of extra trouble.  Why not just:

1. Halt the system.

2. Boot to single-user mode:

   >>> boot -fl s

3. Re-mount / read/write and mount /usr in one fell swoop:

   # /sbin/bcheckrc

   (note: if running LSM, do '/sbin/lsmbstartup' first.)

4. Change root's password:

   # passwd
   New password: blah
   Verification: blah

5. Continue boot to multi-user mode:

   # init 3

